+Where T7 asks whether the pattern lands *inside* the outline, T9 asks whether
+it lands on the *right* face — a different axis, and one point-in-polygon
+cannot see:
+
+  T9  **A drawn cell names a base face, and the scaffold is the set of faces
+      that exist.** bikar stamps every scaffold outline with `data-orb-base-face`
+      — one path, one face of the base solid — and every drawn cell inherits the
+      same label from the face it was copied onto. So the labels the frames
+      carry are two claims a viewer never sees spelled out, and the gate holds
+      them to each other:
+        * *Every face a cell claims is a face the scaffold draws.* the set of
+          base faces the drawn cells name (P) is a subset of the set the
+          scaffold outlines (S). A cell labelled with a face the scaffold never
+          drew is a copy placed on a facet that is not on this solid — the
+          exact defect T7 waves through, because a mislabelled cell sitting
+          geometrically inside the right ring passes point-in-polygon and fails
+          here.
+        * *Every label is a real face.* both sets live in `[0, base.faces)`.
+          An index at or past the face count is a stale renderer writing labels
+          the manifest's own `base` block contradicts.
+        * *A scaffold that draws rings draws labels.* if a base or stage frame
+          carries scaffold outlines but not one `data-orb-base-face` among
+          them, the dist that emitted it predates the stamp — the gate would
+          otherwise read an empty S and pass every later frame vacuously, so
+          the absence is the finding.
+      Strand and morph frames are exempt from the subset check the same way
+      they are exempt from T7's containment: a drawn ribbon spans many faces
+      and carries `data-strand`, not a single base face, and a morph frame is
+      a bend, not a placement. T9 reads only the labels the cell frames stamp,
+      so it needs no geometry and no source — the marks are the whole claim.
+
